00:06:27.670 --> 00:06:29.320
But do you want to
tell us a little bit

00:06:29.320 --> 00:06:31.370
about who both of you are
and what it is you do?

00:06:31.370 --> 00:06:32.370
Sivan, you want to go first?

00:06:32.370 --> 00:06:33.411
SIVAN ALDOR-NOIMAN: Sure.

00:06:33.411 --> 00:06:36.591
So my name's Sivan, and it's
a nice Israeli name, Hebrew

00:06:36.591 --> 00:06:37.090
name--

00:06:37.090 --> 00:06:38.280
Hebrew month.

00:06:38.280 --> 00:06:39.670
My background's in statistics.

00:06:39.670 --> 00:06:43.720
So I have a master and a
PhD in the statistics world.

00:06:43.720 --> 00:06:47.470
And I was kind of intrigued
in applying statistics

00:06:47.470 --> 00:06:49.390
to temporal spatial
models, so that's

00:06:49.390 --> 00:06:50.680
where I started my career.

00:06:50.680 --> 00:06:54.760
And I worked for several years
in the agriculture technology

00:06:54.760 --> 00:06:56.680
space alongside Erik, actually.

00:06:56.680 --> 00:07:00.790
And after several years there
I transitioned to Wellio.

00:07:00.790 --> 00:07:03.850
And in Wellio, we work on the
food technology space, which

00:07:03.850 --> 00:07:06.170
is kind of in a similar space.

00:07:06.170 --> 00:07:06.670
Right?

00:07:06.670 --> 00:07:08.253
They're related to
each other, but one

00:07:08.253 --> 00:07:11.450
tackles more the demand side
and the other the supply side.

00:07:11.450 --> 00:07:13.990
So after several years in
the supply side of food,

00:07:13.990 --> 00:07:15.490
I really wanted
to move to demand.

00:07:15.490 --> 00:07:17.230
Because if you want
to change supply,

00:07:17.230 --> 00:07:19.480
you really should
just change demand.

00:07:19.480 --> 00:07:20.720
That's much easier.

00:07:20.720 --> 00:07:25.150
So I joined Wellio, and I've
been developing models for

00:07:25.150 --> 00:07:27.277
and recommendation
systems for food.

00:07:27.277 --> 00:07:29.110
And I think we'll tell
you a little bit more

00:07:29.110 --> 00:07:30.630
about Wellio in a minute.

00:07:30.630 --> 00:07:32.770
But, yeah, I build
models for life.

00:07:32.770 --> 00:07:36.100
I am a super-duper
happy statistician,

00:07:36.100 --> 00:07:39.120
data scientist/machine learner
and all the words above that

00:07:39.120 --> 00:07:40.720
describe the same position.

00:07:40.720 --> 00:07:42.220
MARK MANDEL: And
you like chocolate.

00:07:42.220 --> 00:07:44.770
SIVAN ALDOR-NOIMAN: And
I love dark chocolate.

00:07:44.770 --> 00:07:46.980
Correct.

00:07:46.980 --> 00:07:48.720
ERIK ANDREJKO: And I'm Erik.

00:07:48.720 --> 00:07:51.310
I'm CTO and
co-founder of Wellio.

00:07:51.310 --> 00:07:53.890
My background is very similar
to Sivan's in some ways.

00:07:53.890 --> 00:07:55.930
I am trained as a
computer scientist.

00:07:55.930 --> 00:07:58.090
I did a PhD, actually,
in pure math.

00:07:58.090 --> 00:08:00.499
I think of myself as a
disillusioned mathematician.

00:08:00.499 --> 00:08:02.290
I got a little tired
of staring at the wall

00:08:02.290 --> 00:08:04.550
and wanted to do something
a little more impactful.

00:08:04.550 --> 00:08:06.670
So I came out to the
Bay Area, and I've

00:08:06.670 --> 00:08:09.570
been working in machine
learning ever since.

00:08:09.570 --> 00:08:13.080
And Wellio kind of grew
out of a personal problem

00:08:13.080 --> 00:08:16.630
that I had that I think is
shared by many people, which

00:08:16.630 --> 00:08:20.650
is to feed my family
at home healthy meals

00:08:20.650 --> 00:08:22.950
and make good
decisions about food

00:08:22.950 --> 00:08:25.660
in ways that make eating at
home the easiest and healthiest

00:08:25.660 --> 00:08:26.240
choice.

00:08:26.240 --> 00:08:29.190
And especially when making
decisions under duress,

00:08:29.190 --> 00:08:31.753
like with a crying
kid and hungry,

00:08:31.753 --> 00:08:34.419
that's exactly the moment when I
want machines to make decisions

00:08:34.419 --> 00:08:36.520
for me because I know I
won't make a good one.

00:08:36.520 --> 00:08:39.280
And I had all this
exposure to technology

00:08:39.280 --> 00:08:42.400
that was helping make
nutrition decisions for plants,

00:08:42.400 --> 00:08:45.380
but I had none of that
technology for my own family.

00:08:45.380 --> 00:08:47.410
So I wanted to
create the experience

00:08:47.410 --> 00:08:50.330
of having a personal chef
and nutritionist in the house

00:08:50.330 --> 00:08:52.952
to help with meal
planning and grocery

00:08:52.952 --> 00:08:54.410
shopping and those
sorts of things.

00:08:54.410 --> 00:08:57.670
And I imagined a way in
which that could be embodied

00:08:57.670 --> 00:08:59.110
in an intelligent machine.

00:08:59.110 --> 00:09:01.170
And so that's kind of
how we got to here.

00:09:01.170 --> 00:09:03.670
SIVAN ALDOR-NOIMAN: When we
talk about Wellio we talk about,

00:09:03.670 --> 00:09:05.800
how can we make a
personalized family

00:09:05.800 --> 00:09:08.590
nutritionist available
to everyone in the world,

00:09:08.590 --> 00:09:10.850
not just someone
that can afford it?

00:09:10.850 --> 00:09:11.780
We both have families.

00:09:11.780 --> 00:09:12.790
I have two kids.

00:09:12.790 --> 00:09:14.740
So we've tackled the
problem of, like, how

00:09:14.740 --> 00:09:16.330
do you feed everyone
in the family?

00:09:16.330 --> 00:09:17.770
How do you personalize it?

00:09:17.770 --> 00:09:18.640
I'm on a diet.

00:09:18.640 --> 00:09:20.380
My husband likes
these kind of foods.

00:09:20.380 --> 00:09:22.360
My kids don't eat
these types of foods.

00:09:22.360 --> 00:09:25.630
So how do we really cook at
home so that everyone's happy,

00:09:25.630 --> 00:09:26.770
everyone's healthy?

00:09:26.770 --> 00:09:28.690
And also the other
side is, how do we

00:09:28.690 --> 00:09:31.780
make people learn the
relationship between food

00:09:31.780 --> 00:09:33.070
and their well-being?

00:09:33.070 --> 00:09:36.250
Which is very hard;
it's very hard to know.

00:09:36.250 --> 00:09:38.470
And you look at trends
of how people have

00:09:38.470 --> 00:09:40.660
thought about diets over time.

00:09:40.660 --> 00:09:41.661
It changes.

00:09:41.661 --> 00:09:42.160
Right?

00:09:42.160 --> 00:09:45.230
One decade, it's you need more
vitamin C. In the next decade,

00:09:45.230 --> 00:09:48.640
it's vitamin D and like, oh,
eat more meat, eat less meat.

00:09:48.640 --> 00:09:51.130
There's not a lot of consistent
science in this domain

00:09:51.130 --> 00:09:53.830
and definitely very hard
to personalize that domain.

00:09:53.830 --> 00:09:56.110
And that's sort of the problem
we're trying to tackle.

00:09:56.110 --> 00:09:57.710
MELANIE WARRICK: Well,
how does Wellio work?

00:09:57.710 --> 00:09:58.790
ERIK ANDREJKO: That's
a very good question.

00:09:58.790 --> 00:10:00.230
So there's two things to Wellio.

00:10:00.230 --> 00:10:04.330
There's the technology
platform, which is really

00:10:04.330 --> 00:10:07.840
ultimately designed
to be an AI for food,

00:10:07.840 --> 00:10:10.060
if you excuse the buzzword
for this type of audience.

00:10:10.060 --> 00:10:10.810
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:10:10.810 --> 00:10:12.650
What does the user
experience look like?

00:10:12.650 --> 00:10:13.030
ERIK ANDREJKO: Yeah.

00:10:13.030 --> 00:10:14.613
And so on top of the
user experience--

00:10:14.613 --> 00:10:17.860
one user experience we are
testing in the market today

00:10:17.860 --> 00:10:20.820
is-- it's essentially a
virtual culinary assistant.

00:10:20.820 --> 00:10:23.010
So you say something
like, I would

00:10:23.010 --> 00:10:26.860
like to eat shrimp tacos
tonight, and then it knows,

00:10:26.860 --> 00:10:28.890
oh, you probably
want corn tortillas

00:10:28.890 --> 00:10:31.830
because there's someone in
the house who's gluten-free.

00:10:31.830 --> 00:10:34.200
You like these brands.

00:10:34.200 --> 00:10:35.915
You need this
number of servings.

00:10:35.915 --> 00:10:37.290
You'll probably
like this recipe,

00:10:37.290 --> 00:10:39.720
but it needs to be modified a
little bit to make it spicy,

00:10:39.720 --> 00:10:42.000
less spicy, reduce
the fat content,

00:10:42.000 --> 00:10:44.240
increase the protein
content, something like that.

00:10:44.240 --> 00:10:45.810
And the system goes
out, translates

00:10:45.810 --> 00:10:48.830
that high-level request into
a specific grocery order.

00:10:48.830 --> 00:10:50.700
It executes the
grocery order for you,

00:10:50.700 --> 00:10:52.200
and then through
one of our partners

00:10:52.200 --> 00:10:55.980
we deliver a bag of groceries
and customized recipes

00:10:55.980 --> 00:10:57.090
to you the same day.

00:10:57.090 --> 00:11:00.892
So it translates a high-level
intent into dinner tonight.

00:11:00.892 --> 00:11:02.850
MELANIE WARRICK: And what
markets do you serve?

00:11:02.850 --> 00:11:05.385
ERIK ANDREJKO: Right now
we're not publicly available,

00:11:05.385 --> 00:11:08.040
although, you can sign
up for our beta list.

00:11:08.040 --> 00:11:11.070
And we can currently serve
about 60% of the US market.

00:11:11.070 --> 00:11:13.290
We're mostly limited
on availability

00:11:13.290 --> 00:11:14.430
of delivery partner.

00:11:14.430 --> 00:11:16.630
And we partner
with Amazon Fresh,

00:11:16.630 --> 00:11:19.080
Instacart-- people like
that to actually do

00:11:19.080 --> 00:11:21.700
the last mile on delivery.

00:11:21.700 --> 00:11:23.110
MELANIE WARRICK: Nice.

00:11:23.110 --> 00:11:27.090
So in terms of data science,
how does data science

00:11:27.090 --> 00:11:28.480
play a role into this?

00:11:28.480 --> 00:11:32.910
SIVAN ALDOR-NOIMAN: So from
minute zero, data and data

00:11:32.910 --> 00:11:35.274
science played a
really big role.

00:11:35.274 --> 00:11:36.690
So I think you can
imagine you can

00:11:36.690 --> 00:11:38.050
build this company in two ways.

00:11:38.050 --> 00:11:41.190
One is you can start
with very limited meals,

00:11:41.190 --> 00:11:44.550
bring an expert in the
domain, like a chef,

00:11:44.550 --> 00:11:47.370
and start creating very
tiny little recipes

00:11:47.370 --> 00:11:49.050
and start promoting
those recipes.

00:11:49.050 --> 00:11:51.120
And over time you would
increase your database

00:11:51.120 --> 00:11:54.840
and make it bigger and learn
intent and so on and so on.

00:11:54.840 --> 00:11:56.550
We did not go that path.

00:11:56.550 --> 00:11:58.270
We didn't feel that's
a scalable path.

00:11:58.270 --> 00:12:00.390
And really from
the get go we said,

00:12:00.390 --> 00:12:02.920
we really want to understand
the culinary space and the food

00:12:02.920 --> 00:12:03.420
space.

00:12:03.420 --> 00:12:06.070
And in order to do that from,
like, as a data scientist,

00:12:06.070 --> 00:12:08.030
what you first need
is a big data set.

00:12:08.030 --> 00:12:13.800
And so we went out and got a
vast amount of data, recipe

00:12:13.800 --> 00:12:18.660
data, for example, and blog data
on food, and articles on food,

00:12:18.660 --> 00:12:20.970
and we generated a
gigantic database

00:12:20.970 --> 00:12:23.700
with food-related information.

00:12:23.700 --> 00:12:27.260
And then we could start
training models on top of it.

00:12:27.260 --> 00:12:30.030
And when Erik says,
like, translating intent

00:12:30.030 --> 00:12:32.390
to a shopping list
and doing all of that,

00:12:32.390 --> 00:12:34.590
there is a lot of models
that come into play.

00:12:34.590 --> 00:12:37.202
From taking this
unstructured data,

00:12:37.202 --> 00:12:39.660
whether it's the intent or even
if you think about the data

00:12:39.660 --> 00:12:42.240
that we've accumulated-- like,
if you look at a recipe data

00:12:42.240 --> 00:12:46.020
from different varieties and you
really think about not a single

00:12:46.020 --> 00:12:48.120
web site but many,
many web sites--

00:12:48.120 --> 00:12:49.085
it's unstructured data.

00:12:49.085 --> 00:12:50.460
So the first thing
you have to do

00:12:50.460 --> 00:12:53.580
is apply some way
to structure it.

00:12:53.580 --> 00:12:55.480
And we took a very
machine learning,

00:12:55.480 --> 00:12:59.205
deep learning approaches, and
we structured it using models.

00:12:59.205 --> 00:13:00.930
And once the data is
structured, then you

00:13:00.930 --> 00:13:02.850
can start building
recommendation systems

00:13:02.850 --> 00:13:04.620
on top of them that
would recommend

00:13:04.620 --> 00:13:06.840
what recipe, how to
adapt the recipe,

00:13:06.840 --> 00:13:10.231
personalize it to a
certain person or a family.

00:13:10.231 --> 00:13:12.230
Even, like, how to make
it into a shopping list.

00:13:12.230 --> 00:13:14.010
So now that you
have a recipe, how

00:13:14.010 --> 00:13:15.556
do you personalize
the shopping list?

00:13:15.556 --> 00:13:17.430
Maybe it's particular
stores that people like

00:13:17.430 --> 00:13:20.430
or brands or particular
items that they prefer--

00:13:20.430 --> 00:13:23.250
organic, for example,
or traditional.

00:13:23.250 --> 00:13:26.100
So all these
personalizations on top

00:13:26.100 --> 00:13:28.950
is another part where the
machine learning and data

00:13:28.950 --> 00:13:29.914
science comes in.

00:13:29.914 --> 00:13:32.080
MELANIE WARRICK: And where
does your data come from?

00:13:32.080 --> 00:13:34.890
SIVAN ALDOR-NOIMAN: The data
comes from propriety sources

00:13:34.890 --> 00:13:35.807
and also from the web.

00:13:35.807 --> 00:13:36.806
MELANIE WARRICK: Got it.

00:13:36.806 --> 00:13:38.670
SIVAN ALDOR-NOIMAN:
So it's a combo.

00:13:38.670 --> 00:13:40.170
MARK MANDEL: So
when you're doing--

00:13:40.170 --> 00:13:41.790
and this is maybe my
naive understanding

00:13:41.790 --> 00:13:42.820
of the machine learning
side of things--

00:13:42.820 --> 00:13:44.250
but when you're doing
these deep model

00:13:44.250 --> 00:13:46.120
analyses of, like, the
data that you have,

00:13:46.120 --> 00:13:49.020
are you essentially categorizing
it so that people can use it

00:13:49.020 --> 00:13:49.930
or, like, tagging it?

00:13:49.930 --> 00:13:51.150
SIVAN ALDOR-NOIMAN: For
us there's definitely

00:13:51.150 --> 00:13:52.690
a level of structuring.

00:13:52.690 --> 00:13:54.300
So when you think
of a recipe you

00:13:54.300 --> 00:13:56.590
can think of ingredients
and preparation lines.

00:13:56.590 --> 00:13:58.890
So one type of
structuring is to take,

00:13:58.890 --> 00:14:01.320
for example, an ingredient
line and extracting certain

00:14:01.320 --> 00:14:03.130
information from that alone--

00:14:03.130 --> 00:14:06.120
like, for example, if it
says one cup of sugar,

00:14:06.120 --> 00:14:08.850
you would need to know that
sugar is the actual food

00:14:08.850 --> 00:14:11.670
entity that was in
that line, and the cup

00:14:11.670 --> 00:14:15.960
is the unit of measure,
and one is the amount.

00:14:15.960 --> 00:14:18.500
So one is structuring--
that kind of structuring.

00:14:18.500 --> 00:14:21.040
And, yeah-- which is kind of
annotation, things like that.

00:14:21.040 --> 00:14:24.660
And the other one is
deriving additional labels.

00:14:24.660 --> 00:14:28.920
So, for example, cuisine
type, or the type of course,

00:14:28.920 --> 00:14:30.280
or things like that.

00:14:30.280 --> 00:14:33.030
So that's another type
of classification models

00:14:33.030 --> 00:14:34.470
that we have to
develop and write.

00:14:34.470 --> 00:14:34.971
Yeah.

00:14:34.971 --> 00:14:36.970
ERIK ANDREJKO: I mean,
there's some other things

00:14:36.970 --> 00:14:39.390
that we've done that are very
interesting that are more

00:14:39.390 --> 00:14:42.400
on the latent semantics space.

00:14:42.400 --> 00:14:45.600
So, for example, on top of
this parsing of recipes,

00:14:45.600 --> 00:14:47.930
you now have a data
set where you have

00:14:47.930 --> 00:14:49.530
co-occurrence of food terms.

00:14:49.530 --> 00:14:52.800
So parmesan and tomatoes
co-occur quite frequently

00:14:52.800 --> 00:14:55.810
for some reason, and
you can actually build,

00:14:55.810 --> 00:14:57.810
essentially, something
just like word2vec.

00:14:57.810 --> 00:14:59.730
You can build vector
representations

00:14:59.730 --> 00:15:00.870
of these food terms.

00:15:00.870 --> 00:15:03.570
And the vector representations
capture culinary use.

00:15:03.570 --> 00:15:05.940
So if you look in the
vector space for things

00:15:05.940 --> 00:15:07.710
that are very close
to parmesan, you

00:15:07.710 --> 00:15:10.790
find all of the other
Italian cheeses--

00:15:10.790 --> 00:15:12.210
Reggiano, et cetera, et cetera.

00:15:12.210 --> 00:15:15.600
And so by kind of taking this
even semi-structured data

00:15:15.600 --> 00:15:18.080
and then using some of
the techniques we all know

00:15:18.080 --> 00:15:21.440
and love, you can get pretty
rich representations that you

00:15:21.440 --> 00:15:24.219
can then use as good signals
or features for other machine

00:15:24.219 --> 00:15:25.760
learning models that
maybe are more--

00:15:25.760 --> 00:15:26.060
SIVAN ALDOR-NOIMAN: Yeah.

00:15:26.060 --> 00:15:27.950
For example, ingredient
substitutions.

00:15:27.950 --> 00:15:28.970
You can think about it that way.

00:15:28.970 --> 00:15:29.470
Right?

00:15:29.470 --> 00:15:32.660
You can think of
generative recipes,

00:15:32.660 --> 00:15:34.997
all sorts of these kind of
applications on top of it.

00:15:34.997 --> 00:15:37.080
MELANIE WARRICK: Are you
doing generative recipes?

00:15:37.080 --> 00:15:38.330
SIVAN ALDOR-NOIMAN:
Uh, I would not

00:15:38.330 --> 00:15:40.260
say that we've conquered
generative recipes.

00:15:40.260 --> 00:15:41.060
MELANIE WARRICK: But
you're exploring it.

00:15:41.060 --> 00:15:41.832
SIVAN ALDOR-NOIMAN:
But we're exploring it.

00:15:41.832 --> 00:15:41.985
Yeah.

00:15:41.985 --> 00:15:42.901
MELANIE WARRICK: Yeah.

00:15:42.901 --> 00:15:45.260
Especially from ingredients--
I know for people who--

00:15:45.260 --> 00:15:48.206
they may have certain types
of ingredients in their house,

00:15:48.206 --> 00:15:49.830
and some of these
recipes can be very--

00:15:49.830 --> 00:15:50.200
ERIK ANDREJKO: Exactly.

00:15:50.200 --> 00:15:51.200
MELANIE WARRICK: Like,
a barrier to entry

00:15:51.200 --> 00:15:53.408
sometimes, with some of the
ingredients they ask for.

00:15:53.408 --> 00:15:55.740
It's nice when you can
see what's easy to swap.

00:15:55.740 --> 00:15:55.952
ERIK ANDREJKO: Yeah.

00:15:55.952 --> 00:15:57.910
And I mean, there's also
very practical things.

00:15:57.910 --> 00:16:00.620
Because oftentimes you go to the
grocery store and the grocery

00:16:00.620 --> 00:16:03.020
stores themselves don't know
what inventory they have.

00:16:03.020 --> 00:16:03.685
MELANIE WARRICK: Yeah.

00:16:03.685 --> 00:16:05.360
ERIK ANDREJKO: And they may
have run out of the thing

00:16:05.360 --> 00:16:07.040
that you intended
to buy, so you need

00:16:07.040 --> 00:16:08.970
to make a decision in the
moment about what to do.

00:16:08.970 --> 00:16:09.928
MELANIE WARRICK: Right.

00:16:09.928 --> 00:16:12.745
MARK MANDEL: And so once you've
got the data set for the food,

00:16:12.745 --> 00:16:14.787
essentially, and the
ingredients and the recipes,

00:16:14.787 --> 00:16:16.453
I'm guessing there's
another side, which

00:16:16.453 --> 00:16:18.160
is like the consumer's
wants and needs.

00:16:18.160 --> 00:16:20.460
And how do you mush the
two things together?

00:16:20.460 --> 00:16:20.840
SIVAN ALDOR-NOIMAN: Yeah.

00:16:20.840 --> 00:16:22.340
That's the personalization step.

00:16:22.340 --> 00:16:22.840
Right?

00:16:22.840 --> 00:16:25.800
So there's kind of two types
that Erik mentioned when he

00:16:25.800 --> 00:16:27.080
sort of described the journey.

00:16:27.080 --> 00:16:30.710
One is more about, like,
the type of things I like,

00:16:30.710 --> 00:16:31.820
my taste.

00:16:31.820 --> 00:16:34.250
I like these types
of recipes, or I'm

00:16:34.250 --> 00:16:35.760
following a certain diet.

00:16:35.760 --> 00:16:36.260
Right?

00:16:36.260 --> 00:16:37.290
Paleo or keto--

00:16:37.290 --> 00:16:38.620
MELANIE WARRICK: Diabetes.

00:16:38.620 --> 00:16:39.800
SIVAN ALDOR-NOIMAN: There's
the health conditions

00:16:39.800 --> 00:16:42.270
that could sort of also
dictate certain things.

00:16:42.270 --> 00:16:45.590
And then there's-- like, that's
more on the food domain when

00:16:45.590 --> 00:16:46.470
you ask people.

00:16:46.470 --> 00:16:49.010
And then there's more on the
shopping domain, which is like,

00:16:49.010 --> 00:16:51.320
what kind of stores
do I like to shop in?

00:16:51.320 --> 00:16:54.465
And what kind of grocery
items do I usually buy?

00:16:54.465 --> 00:16:56.180
What my budget is.

00:16:56.180 --> 00:16:58.340
I have other kind of
constraints that may come in.

00:16:58.340 --> 00:17:00.080
Or what do I have in my pantry?

00:17:00.080 --> 00:17:01.750
And how do I bring it?

00:17:01.750 --> 00:17:03.390
Or, I dislike these types--

00:17:03.390 --> 00:17:04.560
like, I hate pickles.

00:17:04.560 --> 00:17:07.545
So if you bring me a
recipe with pickles,

00:17:07.545 --> 00:17:09.670
you're going to have to
recommend some alternatives

00:17:09.670 --> 00:17:10.859
to those pickles.

00:17:10.859 --> 00:17:12.800
So there's all this information.

00:17:12.800 --> 00:17:14.810
Some of it we can ask directly.

00:17:14.810 --> 00:17:17.670
A lot of other companies do,
like, very, very directly.

00:17:17.670 --> 00:17:18.170
Right?

00:17:18.170 --> 00:17:20.599
Like a diet-- are you
on a specific diet?

00:17:20.599 --> 00:17:22.310
Dislikes and likes,
things like that.

00:17:22.310 --> 00:17:25.324
And others, I think, we've
taken a more different approach.

00:17:25.324 --> 00:17:26.990
Like, for example,
taste for me-- people

00:17:26.990 --> 00:17:29.330
can describe what
they like, but it's

00:17:29.330 --> 00:17:32.690
much easier and maybe much
more informative to not ask it

00:17:32.690 --> 00:17:34.790
directly but indirectly
by asking them

00:17:34.790 --> 00:17:37.280
to choose a set of
recipes from a list,

00:17:37.280 --> 00:17:39.710
or to learn it over time
because they're selecting

00:17:39.710 --> 00:17:43.100
more and more recipes so you can
over time adapt to their taste

00:17:43.100 --> 00:17:47.030
better, or explore their taste
varieties that they like.

00:17:47.030 --> 00:17:50.060
As opposed to asking if
you prefer savory or sweet

00:17:50.060 --> 00:17:50.900
or things like that.

00:17:50.900 --> 00:17:53.900
People may say it, but
they may not always

00:17:53.900 --> 00:17:55.700
exactly want that one thing.

00:17:55.700 --> 00:17:57.410
They may want to venture out.

00:17:57.410 --> 00:18:00.410
So some things we collect
very directly by asking,

00:18:00.410 --> 00:18:02.750
and others kind of indirectly
through the interaction

00:18:02.750 --> 00:18:03.920
with the app itself.

00:18:03.920 --> 00:18:04.670
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:18:04.670 --> 00:18:05.240
That makes sense.

00:18:05.240 --> 00:18:07.070
I'm just thinking, like,
if you asked me directly

00:18:07.070 --> 00:18:08.390
what movies I watch,
I would probably

00:18:08.390 --> 00:18:10.136
tell you arthouse
because I am cultured.

00:18:10.136 --> 00:18:11.510
But if I'm at
home, I am probably

00:18:11.510 --> 00:18:13.202
watching "Terminator 2."

00:18:13.202 --> 00:18:14.480
[LAUGHTER]

00:18:14.480 --> 00:18:15.700
MELANIE WARRICK: Or "Glow."

00:18:15.700 --> 00:18:18.070
Anyways-- no plugging there.

00:18:18.070 --> 00:18:20.610
So this is great.

00:18:20.610 --> 00:18:23.386
What are some of the GCP
products that you're using?

00:18:23.386 --> 00:18:25.010
ERIK ANDREJKO: I
think it may be easier

00:18:25.010 --> 00:18:27.814
to say which GCP
products we're not using.

00:18:27.814 --> 00:18:28.730
MELANIE WARRICK: Sure.

00:18:28.730 --> 00:18:29.360
SIVAN ALDOR-NOIMAN: Yeah.

00:18:29.360 --> 00:18:30.776
MELANIE WARRICK:
That's a new one.

00:18:30.776 --> 00:18:31.689
Let's try that.

00:18:31.689 --> 00:18:33.230
ERIK ANDREJKO: What
are we not using?

00:18:33.230 --> 00:18:33.680
SIVAN ALDOR-NOIMAN: BigTable.

00:18:33.680 --> 00:18:34.760
ERIK ANDREJKO: We're
not using Bigtable.

00:18:34.760 --> 00:18:35.520
That's about it.

00:18:35.520 --> 00:18:36.432
MELANIE WARRICK: Oh, wow.

00:18:36.432 --> 00:18:37.190
So you're using everything else?

00:18:37.190 --> 00:18:38.010
ERIK ANDREJKO: We're
not using Bigtable.

00:18:38.010 --> 00:18:39.200
We're not using Spanner.

00:18:39.200 --> 00:18:40.610
MELANIE WARRICK:
Do you use TPUs?

00:18:40.610 --> 00:18:41.130
ERIK ANDREJKO: Yes.

00:18:41.130 --> 00:18:43.040
MARK MANDEL: Why don't we
start on the journey where

00:18:43.040 --> 00:18:45.248
you started, which is, like,
the data side of things.

00:18:45.248 --> 00:18:47.889
So where does that data go
and how do you process it?

00:18:47.889 --> 00:18:48.680
ERIK ANDREJKO: Yes.

00:18:48.680 --> 00:18:50.388
Actually, so the first
thing we did build

00:18:50.388 --> 00:18:51.920
was the crawling system.

00:18:51.920 --> 00:18:54.147
Because I'm a data scientist.

00:18:54.147 --> 00:18:55.730
I've worked with
many data scientists.

00:18:55.730 --> 00:18:58.820
I've never met a data scientist
who didn't want more data.

00:18:58.820 --> 00:19:00.350
So I figured we'd
better have data

00:19:00.350 --> 00:19:01.683
if we're going to be successful.

00:19:01.683 --> 00:19:03.920
So we built a crawling system
that's built in Python.

00:19:03.920 --> 00:19:07.430
The actual operations
of it is heavily

00:19:07.430 --> 00:19:10.460
influenced by the availability
of things like GKE.

00:19:10.460 --> 00:19:13.370
That runs on GKE
autoscales on GKE.

00:19:13.370 --> 00:19:16.370
And all of the data coming out
of that is dumped on to GCS.

00:19:16.370 --> 00:19:17.960
And then all of
the post-processing

00:19:17.960 --> 00:19:20.060
happens via Pub/Sub.

00:19:20.060 --> 00:19:21.850
So there's various stages.

00:19:21.850 --> 00:19:25.080
It goes through kind of a stage
pipeline that's all coordinated

00:19:25.080 --> 00:19:28.520
by my Pub/Sub, and there
are various GKE clusters

00:19:28.520 --> 00:19:29.734
that read the messages--

00:19:29.734 --> 00:19:32.150
SIVAN ALDOR-NOIMAN: It's also
stored on BigQuery even when

00:19:32.150 --> 00:19:32.930
it's unstructured.

00:19:32.930 --> 00:19:33.763
ERIK ANDREJKO: Yeah.

00:19:33.763 --> 00:19:36.630
There's some-- a whole bunch
of side channels that--

00:19:36.630 --> 00:19:38.990
out of this pipeline
we collect data

00:19:38.990 --> 00:19:40.850
at various levels
of normalization.

00:19:40.850 --> 00:19:43.190
That is available
on GCS in some rawer

00:19:43.190 --> 00:19:46.800
form, which we can then access,
and we do access via Dataproc.

00:19:46.800 --> 00:19:49.280
But primarily it
ends up in BigQuery.

00:19:49.280 --> 00:19:51.440
And then most of the
machine learning,

00:19:51.440 --> 00:19:55.460
either kind of in an ad hoc
or in a more formal repeated

00:19:55.460 --> 00:19:55.960
training.

00:19:55.960 --> 00:19:58.293
MELANIE WARRICK: Are you using
Dataproc with more, like,

00:19:58.293 --> 00:19:59.787
a Hadoop or with a Spark or--

00:19:59.787 --> 00:20:00.870
ERIK ANDREJKO: With Spark.

00:20:00.870 --> 00:20:01.250
MELANIE WARRICK: Got it.

00:20:01.250 --> 00:20:02.083
ERIK ANDREJKO: Yeah.

00:20:02.083 --> 00:20:04.250
Actually, we're increasingly
kind of automating

00:20:04.250 --> 00:20:07.580
a lot of our model training
evaluation and deployment

00:20:07.580 --> 00:20:08.750
pipelines on Composer.

00:20:08.750 --> 00:20:10.250
MARK MANDEL: Tell
us about Composer,

00:20:10.250 --> 00:20:11.762
because I think that's very new.

00:20:11.762 --> 00:20:13.220
In fact, I have no
idea what it is.

00:20:13.220 --> 00:20:14.803
ERIK ANDREJKO:
Composer is essentially

00:20:14.803 --> 00:20:16.190
an orchestration layer.

00:20:16.190 --> 00:20:18.350
It's hosted Airflow.

00:20:18.350 --> 00:20:21.211
Airflow seems to have
been increasingly

00:20:21.211 --> 00:20:23.460
widely-used in the industry
for doing machine learning

00:20:23.460 --> 00:20:27.510
pipelines where I have-- step A,
I transform some data; step B,

00:20:27.510 --> 00:20:29.460
I maybe need to train a model.

00:20:29.460 --> 00:20:31.650
Next step is maybe I do a
model evaluation, and then

00:20:31.650 --> 00:20:34.450
my model selection, and
then I need to deploy it.

00:20:34.450 --> 00:20:36.420
So if you want to
run model training

00:20:36.420 --> 00:20:37.860
on some schedule
like that, we've

00:20:37.860 --> 00:20:40.394
been doing that
recently in Composer,

00:20:40.394 --> 00:20:41.560
and that's worked very well.

00:20:41.560 --> 00:20:44.970
There's a lot of nice hooks
for some of the other GCP

00:20:44.970 --> 00:20:47.010
offerings, for instance
Cloud ML Engine.

00:20:47.010 --> 00:20:50.730
We use a lot of Cloud ML
Engine with Keras, actually,

00:20:50.730 --> 00:20:51.390
increasingly.

00:20:51.390 --> 00:20:56.190
And we launch our training
jobs directly from Composer

00:20:56.190 --> 00:20:58.770
as we get new data coming in--

00:20:58.770 --> 00:21:01.890
new label data or
new unstructured data

00:21:01.890 --> 00:21:03.130
depending on the pipeline.

00:21:03.130 --> 00:21:03.450
SIVAN ALDOR-NOIMAN: Yeah.

00:21:03.450 --> 00:21:04.866
And we do have
several models that

00:21:04.866 --> 00:21:08.640
really are kind of continuous
integration development

00:21:08.640 --> 00:21:12.270
and deployment in the sense
that we've developed them.

00:21:12.270 --> 00:21:13.410
We train them.

00:21:13.410 --> 00:21:15.660
We get all of that
from Composer.

00:21:15.660 --> 00:21:17.760
And then there is
a human in the loop

00:21:17.760 --> 00:21:20.880
that once the output
of the model is shown,

00:21:20.880 --> 00:21:23.780
we have an internal tool that
would show the model output.

00:21:23.780 --> 00:21:25.740
Internally in our
team we have experts--

00:21:25.740 --> 00:21:29.070
domain experts, chefs, and
people in the culinary space--

00:21:29.070 --> 00:21:32.020
that would review the labels
that the model produced.

00:21:32.020 --> 00:21:33.960
For example, like,
we talked about it

00:21:33.960 --> 00:21:36.920
could say the type of
cuisine or things like that.

00:21:36.920 --> 00:21:40.370
And they would correct, and we
would collect that corrected

00:21:40.370 --> 00:21:43.350
data, and then we
can sort of train

00:21:43.350 --> 00:21:46.860
again the model with more data
so that over time the model

00:21:46.860 --> 00:21:47.630
improves.

00:21:47.630 --> 00:21:49.950
And we can monitor
that improvement.

00:21:49.950 --> 00:21:52.260
So we definitely have
kind of those mechanisms.

00:21:52.260 --> 00:21:54.260
MARK MANDEL: That's
actually really interesting.

00:21:54.260 --> 00:21:55.410
So you're not just ML.

00:21:55.410 --> 00:21:56.640
And, as you said
before, you were like,

00:21:56.640 --> 00:21:58.015
we're not just
going to do human.

00:21:58.015 --> 00:21:59.680
It's kind of the
combination of both,

00:21:59.680 --> 00:22:00.030
or a little bit of tweaking?

00:22:00.030 --> 00:22:00.690
SIVAN ALDOR-NOIMAN: Yeah.

00:22:00.690 --> 00:22:02.400
MELANIE WARRICK: A lot of
all companies do that, too.

00:22:02.400 --> 00:22:02.730
SIVAN ALDOR-NOIMAN: Yeah.

00:22:02.730 --> 00:22:04.470
I think we-- even
with our experience

00:22:04.470 --> 00:22:07.380
from our previous company,
it's very clear to us

00:22:07.380 --> 00:22:10.560
that machine learnings
and models-- and, I guess,

00:22:10.560 --> 00:22:11.940
that's also my background.

00:22:11.940 --> 00:22:15.930
It's like, I cannot in good
conscience have a model that I

00:22:15.930 --> 00:22:17.977
believe will always be right.

00:22:17.977 --> 00:22:19.560
There's always going
to be cases where

00:22:19.560 --> 00:22:21.220
a model's going to be wrong.

00:22:21.220 --> 00:22:23.070
And, as Erik said,
as a data scientist

00:22:23.070 --> 00:22:24.730
I'm always going
to want more data.

00:22:24.730 --> 00:22:27.600
So having experts in
the room talking to you,

00:22:27.600 --> 00:22:29.830
helping you understand the
data-- and I would admit,

00:22:29.830 --> 00:22:33.240
I cooked before coming to
Wellio, but I am not a chef,

00:22:33.240 --> 00:22:35.340
and I am not an
expert in this domain.

00:22:35.340 --> 00:22:37.659
So having that domain
expert sit next to me

00:22:37.659 --> 00:22:39.450
and asking lots of
questions about the data

00:22:39.450 --> 00:22:42.540
so that I understand how to
properly utilize it really

00:22:42.540 --> 00:22:43.520
helps.

00:22:43.520 --> 00:22:45.710
And so, yeah, we've
devised the mechanism

00:22:45.710 --> 00:22:50.670
so that we can kind of get the
most from this domain expert

00:22:50.670 --> 00:22:51.695
and scale in some sense.

00:22:51.695 --> 00:22:53.070
MELANIE WARRICK:
When the data is

00:22:53.070 --> 00:22:57.150
being flowed through your
ETL and through the data

00:22:57.150 --> 00:22:58.750
and doing the training,
is it batched?

00:22:58.750 --> 00:23:00.660
Is it streaming?

00:23:00.660 --> 00:23:01.599
Is it a mix?

00:23:01.599 --> 00:23:02.890
SIVAN ALDOR-NOIMAN: It's a mix.

00:23:02.890 --> 00:23:03.090
ERIK ANDREJKO: Yeah.

00:23:03.090 --> 00:23:03.660
It's a mix.

00:23:03.660 --> 00:23:05.850
It's primarily batch
today, especially

00:23:05.850 --> 00:23:07.220
in the training side.

00:23:07.220 --> 00:23:10.260
I think going forward it'll
be more online and streaming.

00:23:10.260 --> 00:23:13.820
We do do streaming in
another way in which we do

00:23:13.820 --> 00:23:14.610
human-in-the-loop.

00:23:14.610 --> 00:23:16.359
And that is to have
human-in-the-loop kind

00:23:16.359 --> 00:23:17.380
of operationally.

00:23:17.380 --> 00:23:20.760
So we have a system
where a model

00:23:20.760 --> 00:23:24.570
will be selecting a grocery
list, say, for an individual.

00:23:24.570 --> 00:23:27.780
And then the model will also
output some confidence scores

00:23:27.780 --> 00:23:29.760
with respect to the
rankings it's made

00:23:29.760 --> 00:23:31.290
in terms of the grocery items.

00:23:31.290 --> 00:23:33.690
And then if that falls
below some threshold,

00:23:33.690 --> 00:23:36.540
we actually will shunt some
that work to a human expert,

00:23:36.540 --> 00:23:39.210
and the human expert
can review and override

00:23:39.210 --> 00:23:40.950
some of the
model-based decisions.

00:23:40.950 --> 00:23:43.554
And that's entirely
streaming-- that process.

00:23:43.554 --> 00:23:44.470
MELANIE WARRICK: Nice.

00:23:44.470 --> 00:23:45.511
SIVAN ALDOR-NOIMAN: Yeah.

00:23:45.511 --> 00:23:47.520
I would say we have
sufficient data that we

00:23:47.520 --> 00:23:49.890
don't get a lot from
training online,

00:23:49.890 --> 00:23:51.720
like, from every observation.

00:23:51.720 --> 00:23:54.570
We probably do need
enough quantity

00:23:54.570 --> 00:23:56.661
to sort of move
the needle again.

00:23:56.661 --> 00:23:59.160
Because the needle is already
kind of quite high, so to move

00:23:59.160 --> 00:24:00.880
it again takes a while.

00:24:00.880 --> 00:24:03.450
And so I think the system
that Erik describes helps

00:24:03.450 --> 00:24:06.000
us deal with outliers
and things that

00:24:06.000 --> 00:24:08.790
don't work all the way like
it should-- because they're

00:24:08.790 --> 00:24:11.370
outlier, and they will take
time for the machine learning

00:24:11.370 --> 00:24:14.284
to sort of get all those
kind of edge cases.

00:24:14.284 --> 00:24:16.200
MELANIE WARRICK: And I'm
also curious in terms

00:24:16.200 --> 00:24:18.240
of your setup, are you using--

00:24:18.240 --> 00:24:20.960
from the more data-engineering
side of things--

00:24:20.960 --> 00:24:23.050
Stackdriver and other
performance tools, too?

00:24:23.050 --> 00:24:23.150
ERIK ANDREJKO: Oh, yeah.

00:24:23.150 --> 00:24:23.360
SIVAN ALDOR-NOIMAN: Yeah.

00:24:23.360 --> 00:24:24.270
Yeah.

00:24:24.270 --> 00:24:26.992
MELANIE WARRICK: To make
sure the system's functioning

00:24:26.992 --> 00:24:27.950
the way you want it to?

00:24:27.950 --> 00:24:28.991
SIVAN ALDOR-NOIMAN: Yeah.

00:24:28.991 --> 00:24:31.907
When Erik said, you know,
ask us what we're not using,

00:24:31.907 --> 00:24:32.740
it's actually right.

00:24:32.740 --> 00:24:33.564
It's probably true.

00:24:33.564 --> 00:24:35.730
You know, there's a
presentation with all the Google

00:24:35.730 --> 00:24:36.540
icons in them.

00:24:36.540 --> 00:24:36.980
ERIK ANDREJKO: Yeah.

00:24:36.980 --> 00:24:39.280
SIVAN ALDOR-NOIMAN: But I
think we can highlight many,

00:24:39.280 --> 00:24:40.630
many of the icons that we use.

00:24:40.630 --> 00:24:43.050
We're really-- not just
the data science but also

00:24:43.050 --> 00:24:46.890
the engineering side-- very
adventurous and up-to-date

00:24:46.890 --> 00:24:48.900
with the current
technology that Google

00:24:48.900 --> 00:24:54.060
is offering so that we can
adapt quickly and move fast.

00:24:54.060 --> 00:24:57.460
And I think we've been
really, really good at, like--

00:24:57.460 --> 00:24:59.520
I know a lot of
beta-- like Composer

00:24:59.520 --> 00:25:01.770
and other things
that are in pure beta

00:25:01.770 --> 00:25:05.790
that we are really adapting
already into our stack.

00:25:05.790 --> 00:25:08.280
I got excited in this
conference to hear more

00:25:08.280 --> 00:25:10.440
about Cloud Function
and bringing Python

00:25:10.440 --> 00:25:11.670
into the mix of it.

00:25:11.670 --> 00:25:14.940
So I'm eager to test that
in our infrastructure.

00:25:14.940 --> 00:25:16.080
We don't have that yet.

00:25:16.080 --> 00:25:18.310
But definitely other
components from Google

00:25:18.310 --> 00:25:20.680
has made it very easy,
as a data scientist,

00:25:20.680 --> 00:25:24.890
to sort of explore data,
push models into production.

00:25:24.890 --> 00:25:27.370
It's been a really nice
platform to work with.

00:25:27.370 --> 00:25:28.420
MELANIE WARRICK: And you
mentioned some of the models

00:25:28.420 --> 00:25:30.196
that you're working
with are recommend--

00:25:30.196 --> 00:25:31.570
well, a lot of
these end up being

00:25:31.570 --> 00:25:36.530
some form of recommendation
or classification anyways.

00:25:36.530 --> 00:25:38.710
And it sounds like
you're using ML Engine,

00:25:38.710 --> 00:25:40.270
or you said you were
using ML Engine.

00:25:40.270 --> 00:25:43.191
So you're using
neural nets then?

00:25:43.191 --> 00:25:44.440
SIVAN ALDOR-NOIMAN: Some are--

00:25:44.440 --> 00:25:44.940
yeah.

00:25:44.940 --> 00:25:48.010
Some are, like, deep
learning nets and some are--

00:25:48.010 --> 00:25:50.560
I like to call them shallow
models now that everyone really

00:25:50.560 --> 00:25:53.080
loves the buzzwords
of deep models.

00:25:53.080 --> 00:25:56.830
So, yeah, we use a combination
of shallow and deep model

00:25:56.830 --> 00:25:58.240
depending on the application.

00:25:58.240 --> 00:25:59.800
But a lot of it--

00:25:59.800 --> 00:26:03.610
we talked about-- we have
these semantic representation

00:26:03.610 --> 00:26:06.640
of certain things, and
then we might train a more

00:26:06.640 --> 00:26:08.490
shallow model on top of it.

00:26:08.490 --> 00:26:08.990
Right?

00:26:08.990 --> 00:26:10.760
So Transfer Learn
and things like that.

00:26:10.760 --> 00:26:12.385
So we have a combo
of these approaches.

00:26:12.385 --> 00:26:14.218
MELANIE WARRICK: Are
you using other things,

00:26:14.218 --> 00:26:16.151
like random forests
and linear regressions,

00:26:16.151 --> 00:26:17.650
based on the types
of other problems

00:26:17.650 --> 00:26:18.230
that you're working on?

00:26:18.230 --> 00:26:18.360
SIVAN ALDOR-NOIMAN: Yeah.

00:26:18.360 --> 00:26:18.920
Yeah.

00:26:18.920 --> 00:26:20.260
MELANIE WARRICK: Most
companies would be.

00:26:20.260 --> 00:26:20.270
Yeah.

00:26:20.270 --> 00:26:21.200
SIVAN ALDOR-NOIMAN:
It's a combination.

00:26:21.200 --> 00:26:22.470
I really am not--

00:26:22.470 --> 00:26:27.175
I'm trained as a statistician,
so I'd rather be--

00:26:27.175 --> 00:26:28.260
and I'm practical.

00:26:28.260 --> 00:26:30.910
So I'm like, OK, what
is the right approach

00:26:30.910 --> 00:26:32.080
for this problem?

00:26:32.080 --> 00:26:34.930
And I don't believe the deep
learning is the solution to all

00:26:34.930 --> 00:26:36.560
of the problems in the world.

00:26:36.560 --> 00:26:38.535
I believe that you need
to understand the data.

00:26:38.535 --> 00:26:40.660
You need to understand what
you're trying to solve.

00:26:40.660 --> 00:26:43.390
Sometimes simple
approaches can get you

00:26:43.390 --> 00:26:46.830
what you need in order to
solve the business problem.

00:26:46.830 --> 00:26:50.500
ERIK ANDREJKO: It tends to be a
very layered approach in that--

00:26:50.500 --> 00:26:52.840
like, the models that are
consuming, essentially,

00:26:52.840 --> 00:26:55.210
unstructured data--
that could be raw text,

00:26:55.210 --> 00:26:58.120
that could be
images, for example--

00:26:58.120 --> 00:26:59.500
tend to be deep neural networks.

00:26:59.500 --> 00:27:02.530
And then they output some
vector representation

00:27:02.530 --> 00:27:04.930
or some additional
auxiliary data

00:27:04.930 --> 00:27:08.740
or labeling that then can be
fed into an application-specific

00:27:08.740 --> 00:27:09.250
model.

00:27:09.250 --> 00:27:11.208
And sometimes a model is
a deep neural network,

00:27:11.208 --> 00:27:13.334
but very often it's not.

00:27:13.334 --> 00:27:15.250
MARK MANDEL: How are you
serving these models?

00:27:15.250 --> 00:27:17.670
Like, how do you end up
using them at the end?

00:27:17.670 --> 00:27:18.503
ERIK ANDREJKO: Yeah.

00:27:18.503 --> 00:27:21.419
We serve them-- well, we have
a microservices architecture.

00:27:21.419 --> 00:27:22.960
And so everything,
for the most part,

00:27:22.960 --> 00:27:25.720
ends up in GKE in
some form or another.

00:27:25.720 --> 00:27:27.250
We are starting to
increasingly use

00:27:27.250 --> 00:27:30.670
Cloud ML Engine as a deployment
platform when we can.

00:27:30.670 --> 00:27:32.920
I'm excited to
try that with more

00:27:32.920 --> 00:27:34.580
frameworks than TensorFlow.

00:27:34.580 --> 00:27:35.830
We've done it with TensorFlow.

00:27:35.830 --> 00:27:37.960
I'm excited to try that
outside of TensorFlow.

00:27:37.960 --> 00:27:40.420
Because we like to
think about the models

00:27:40.420 --> 00:27:42.010
we produce as
essentially appliances,

00:27:42.010 --> 00:27:43.926
and then the engineering
organization can just

00:27:43.926 --> 00:27:46.040
use them as any other service.

00:27:46.040 --> 00:27:48.160
It's just another
API that you call.

00:27:48.160 --> 00:27:49.610
We're a very small team.

00:27:49.610 --> 00:27:51.490
We have no platform
engineering team.

00:27:51.490 --> 00:27:53.870
GCP is our platform
engineering team.

00:27:53.870 --> 00:27:55.887
So we either have none
or we have a giant one,

00:27:55.887 --> 00:27:57.220
depending on how you look at it.

00:27:57.220 --> 00:27:59.110
And we have no operations team.

00:27:59.110 --> 00:28:00.990
Everyone just kind
of owns operations,

00:28:00.990 --> 00:28:02.740
and that includes all
the data scientists.

00:28:02.740 --> 00:28:05.910
So all the machine learning
operations, online models

00:28:05.910 --> 00:28:08.590
monitoring, performance
monitoring of Stackdriver,

00:28:08.590 --> 00:28:10.537
for example, that's
all done by--

00:28:10.537 --> 00:28:11.620
SIVAN ALDOR-NOIMAN: By us.

00:28:11.620 --> 00:28:12.420
ERIK ANDREJKO: Yeah.

00:28:12.420 --> 00:28:12.910
By all of us.

00:28:12.910 --> 00:28:13.890
SIVAN ALDOR-NOIMAN: By
our data scientists.

00:28:13.890 --> 00:28:14.690
ERIK ANDREJKO: Yeah.

00:28:14.690 --> 00:28:15.200
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:28:15.200 --> 00:28:16.408
So that's really interesting.

00:28:16.408 --> 00:28:20.640
And you're using a lot of
very different pieces of GCP,

00:28:20.640 --> 00:28:23.050
but you're all learning
it with a small team.

00:28:23.050 --> 00:28:25.265
Are you facing any
challenges about, basically,

00:28:25.265 --> 00:28:26.140
using so many things?

00:28:26.140 --> 00:28:27.266
Or are you finding it nice?

00:28:27.266 --> 00:28:28.515
Or how's that experience like?

00:28:28.515 --> 00:28:30.940
ERIK ANDREJKO: Well, I mean,
some things are kind of new,

00:28:30.940 --> 00:28:34.420
I think, to the data science
industry, in some ways.

00:28:34.420 --> 00:28:37.947
I mean, most times when we
talk to other data scientists,

00:28:37.947 --> 00:28:40.280
they're not super familiar
with Kubernetes, for example.

00:28:40.280 --> 00:28:41.590
So that's just new.

00:28:41.590 --> 00:28:43.600
But I think what
we found is very

00:28:43.600 --> 00:28:46.840
nice is that the
abstraction layers of GCP

00:28:46.840 --> 00:28:50.350
seem to be well-considered in
that you don't have to relearn

00:28:50.350 --> 00:28:54.110
API primitives or command line
interfaces over and over again

00:28:54.110 --> 00:28:56.050
to use different
parts of the system.

00:28:56.050 --> 00:28:57.865
There is a fair
amount of consistency.

00:28:57.865 --> 00:29:00.880
And so once our
engineers or data

00:29:00.880 --> 00:29:04.770
scientists kind of grok the
underlying conceptual space,

00:29:04.770 --> 00:29:07.542
using it tends to
be pretty easy.

00:29:07.542 --> 00:29:10.000
SIVAN ALDOR-NOIMAN: And there's
some learning that you need

00:29:10.000 --> 00:29:12.700
to do in order to ramp up on
the different technologies,

00:29:12.700 --> 00:29:14.362
but I feel like--

00:29:14.362 --> 00:29:17.110
like, I'd never worked on
the Google Cloud Platform

00:29:17.110 --> 00:29:18.240
before joining Wellio.

00:29:18.240 --> 00:29:22.030
And, yeah, it took a while,
but not as much as it took me

00:29:22.030 --> 00:29:23.400
other platforms.

00:29:23.400 --> 00:29:25.550
So that's a good thing.

00:29:25.550 --> 00:29:26.050
[LAUGHTER]

00:29:26.050 --> 00:29:26.990
MARK MANDEL: That's
the sound bite.

00:29:26.990 --> 00:29:27.460
Thank you.

00:29:27.460 --> 00:29:27.960
We're done.

00:29:27.960 --> 00:29:28.870
[LAUGHING]

00:29:28.870 --> 00:29:30.580
MELANIE WARRICK: Two different
questions I want to ask.

00:29:30.580 --> 00:29:31.690
Earlier, before,
you were saying you

00:29:31.690 --> 00:29:33.100
want to try to
make it accessible

00:29:33.100 --> 00:29:34.324
outside of TensorFlow.

00:29:34.324 --> 00:29:36.490
Is there a reason why you
want to be able to explore

00:29:36.490 --> 00:29:37.480
beyond TensorFlow?

00:29:37.480 --> 00:29:39.490
And is there specific
software that you'd

00:29:39.490 --> 00:29:41.119
be more interested in?

00:29:41.119 --> 00:29:43.660
ERIK ANDREJKO: Well, as Sivan
was saying, our, quote unquote,

00:29:43.660 --> 00:29:44.410
"shallow models"--

00:29:44.410 --> 00:29:45.740
I love this
branding, by the way.

00:29:45.740 --> 00:29:47.860
SIVAN ALDOR-NOIMAN: I say it
constantly, shallow and deep.

00:29:47.860 --> 00:29:50.070
I find it very funny
that people call it deep,

00:29:50.070 --> 00:29:51.390
and now there's no alternative.

00:29:51.390 --> 00:29:51.670
ERIK ANDREJKO: Yeah.

00:29:51.670 --> 00:29:52.710
Once you go deep
it's hard to say

00:29:52.710 --> 00:29:53.320
I'm not doing anything deep.

00:29:53.320 --> 00:29:53.760
SIVAN ALDOR-NOIMAN:
It's shallow.

00:29:53.760 --> 00:29:54.395
[LAUGHING]

00:29:54.395 --> 00:29:56.770
ERIK ANDREJKO: So for our
non-deep neural network models,

00:29:56.770 --> 00:29:58.460
they're primarily scikit-learn.

00:29:58.460 --> 00:30:02.290
And so to be able to deploy
those as individual RESTful

00:30:02.290 --> 00:30:03.964
interfaces would be very useful.

00:30:03.964 --> 00:30:05.880
MELANIE WARRICK:
Scikit-learn is very popular.

00:30:05.880 --> 00:30:06.381
Yes.

00:30:06.381 --> 00:30:07.171
ERIK ANDREJKO: Yes.

00:30:07.171 --> 00:30:08.620
Scikit-learn is very popular.

00:30:08.620 --> 00:30:10.070
I think we could
do both of those.

00:30:10.070 --> 00:30:12.880
And then that allows us
also to do model-versioning

00:30:12.880 --> 00:30:15.940
so that we can do A/B testing
or canary deployments.

00:30:15.940 --> 00:30:17.570
That's something we
want to do more of.

00:30:17.570 --> 00:30:19.430
And it provides
us safety nets so

00:30:19.430 --> 00:30:23.200
that we can more quickly deploy
new models for production.

00:30:23.200 --> 00:30:25.880
And we don't have to
be so careful in terms

00:30:25.880 --> 00:30:28.477
of the testing, but we
can deploy and monitor,

00:30:28.477 --> 00:30:30.560
and then we have the safety
to roll if we need to.

00:30:30.560 --> 00:30:31.580
MELANIE WARRICK:
And you were saying

00:30:31.580 --> 00:30:33.746
how it took a little bit
of time to get up to speed.

00:30:33.746 --> 00:30:36.530
Any tips or tricks
or advice for those

00:30:36.530 --> 00:30:38.990
who are exploring GCP
for the first time,

00:30:38.990 --> 00:30:41.610
trying to build out their own
solutions for their company?

00:30:41.610 --> 00:30:44.730
SIVAN ALDOR-NOIMAN: So I was
in a bigger company before.

00:30:44.730 --> 00:30:47.660
So in a bigger company
data scientist-- at least

00:30:47.660 --> 00:30:51.190
in my experience-- they really
have a very specific role.

00:30:51.190 --> 00:30:52.940
They don't really do
the data engineering.

00:30:52.940 --> 00:30:54.106
That's done by someone else.

00:30:54.106 --> 00:30:56.000
They're very, very
data science, meaning

00:30:56.000 --> 00:30:58.250
we build-- you look at the
data, you build a model,

00:30:58.250 --> 00:31:00.220
and you push it to someone else.

00:31:00.220 --> 00:31:02.090
And I think my biggest
problem was like,

00:31:02.090 --> 00:31:04.525
oh, I now need to learn all
the data engineering side

00:31:04.525 --> 00:31:06.150
because no one's
going to do it for me.

00:31:06.150 --> 00:31:08.874
Oh, I need to learn DevOps
because there's no DevOps team.

00:31:08.874 --> 00:31:10.290
Oh, I need to do
all these things.

00:31:10.290 --> 00:31:13.010
So it was more
tackling those things.

00:31:13.010 --> 00:31:15.380
I think from a data
science perspective--

00:31:15.380 --> 00:31:18.260
like, if my role in this
company was just look at data,

00:31:18.260 --> 00:31:20.840
develop models, and
push it, this platform

00:31:20.840 --> 00:31:23.551
makes it extremely
easy to do those tasks.

00:31:23.551 --> 00:31:24.050
Really.

00:31:24.050 --> 00:31:26.450
Like, accessing the data
in different formats

00:31:26.450 --> 00:31:28.430
from different
locations has become

00:31:28.430 --> 00:31:30.500
extremely easy and simple.

00:31:30.500 --> 00:31:33.320
Spinning up your
own notebooks to be

00:31:33.320 --> 00:31:37.010
able to explore the data from
different places is very easy.

00:31:37.010 --> 00:31:39.470
Deploying the model now
becomes extremely easy

00:31:39.470 --> 00:31:40.940
too with Cloud ML.

00:31:40.940 --> 00:31:44.000
So it's a platform that's
designed, in some sense,

00:31:44.000 --> 00:31:46.580
for a data scientist
to play around with.

00:31:46.580 --> 00:31:48.830
I think it becomes more
challenging when you're like,

00:31:48.830 --> 00:31:51.710
yeah, you're also the
engineer and the DevOps

00:31:51.710 --> 00:31:53.642
so you need to learn
about Kubernetes

00:31:53.642 --> 00:31:55.100
and you need-- oh
yeah, you do need

00:31:55.100 --> 00:31:56.990
to understand all these things.

00:31:56.990 --> 00:31:58.920
Then it becomes a little
bit more complicated.

00:31:58.920 --> 00:31:59.870
MELANIE WARRICK: You
also mentioned earlier

00:31:59.870 --> 00:32:01.590
that you are using TPUs.

00:32:01.590 --> 00:32:03.490
How are you using TPUs?

00:32:03.490 --> 00:32:05.240
SIVAN ALDOR-NOIMAN:
For training purposes.

00:32:05.240 --> 00:32:06.073
ERIK ANDREJKO: Yeah.

00:32:06.073 --> 00:32:07.910
Well, one thing that's
very important for us

00:32:07.910 --> 00:32:10.160
is to think about
our biggest costs.

00:32:10.160 --> 00:32:13.140
And our biggest cost
is opportunity cost

00:32:13.140 --> 00:32:15.991
in that we don't have an
infinite number of software

00:32:15.991 --> 00:32:16.490
engineers.

00:32:16.490 --> 00:32:19.660
So if a software engineer is
doing x, they're not doing y.

00:32:19.660 --> 00:32:23.270
And that means-- especially
for people building models--

00:32:23.270 --> 00:32:25.689
that the faster they can
get to the next iteration

00:32:25.689 --> 00:32:26.480
result, the better.

00:32:26.480 --> 00:32:28.070
So that is reducing
training time.

00:32:28.070 --> 00:32:30.440
And we use accelerators
and TPUs and GPUs

00:32:30.440 --> 00:32:33.280
for reducing time to answer.

00:32:33.280 --> 00:32:35.217
And the other thing that
we increasingly use,

00:32:35.217 --> 00:32:36.800
which has been very
beneficial for us,

00:32:36.800 --> 00:32:38.600
is the hyperparameter
optimization.

00:32:38.600 --> 00:32:41.313
So it used to be that we would
have these long discussions.

00:32:41.313 --> 00:32:42.646
SIVAN ALDOR-NOIMAN: That's true.

00:32:42.646 --> 00:32:44.896
ERIK ANDREJKO: People thought
that their job as a data

00:32:44.896 --> 00:32:48.772
scientist was to know and
have intuition about what

00:32:48.772 --> 00:32:49.980
type of parameters to choose.

00:32:49.980 --> 00:32:52.507
And now I tell them, don't
really worry about that.

00:32:52.507 --> 00:32:53.840
We'll just run a big experiment.

00:32:53.840 --> 00:32:55.640
And I'd rather
have the experiment

00:32:55.640 --> 00:32:57.530
running than spend
another couple of days

00:32:57.530 --> 00:32:59.150
thinking about which
parameters should be used.

00:32:59.150 --> 00:33:00.680
MARK MANDEL: What's the issue
there for people who aren't

00:33:00.680 --> 00:33:01.999
familiar with that space?

00:33:01.999 --> 00:33:03.290
ERIK ANDREJKO: When you build--

00:33:03.290 --> 00:33:05.156
say you take a deep
neural network.

00:33:05.156 --> 00:33:07.280
You have to decide how many
layers you should have,

00:33:07.280 --> 00:33:10.702
how big each layer
should be, which

00:33:10.702 --> 00:33:11.910
units you have in each layer.

00:33:11.910 --> 00:33:13.520
You could choose
from different units.

00:33:13.520 --> 00:33:14.540
MELANIE WARRICK:
Activation functions.

00:33:14.540 --> 00:33:15.373
ERIK ANDREJKO: Yeah.

00:33:15.373 --> 00:33:17.270
I mean, there's just
a smorgasbord of--

00:33:17.270 --> 00:33:18.450
MELANIE WARRICK: The way
I like to explain it--

00:33:18.450 --> 00:33:20.783
and I'm just going to jump
in with this-- is it's almost

00:33:20.783 --> 00:33:22.880
like you have one of
those very complex radios

00:33:22.880 --> 00:33:25.550
that you can tune all
the things for the radio

00:33:25.550 --> 00:33:27.740
to make the sound sound
like a certain quality.

00:33:27.740 --> 00:33:30.470
But you've got to be an
expert on how to tune it,

00:33:30.470 --> 00:33:31.730
and that can be a challenge.

00:33:31.730 --> 00:33:32.180
ERIK ANDREJKO: Yeah.

00:33:32.180 --> 00:33:32.990
That can be a challenge.

00:33:32.990 --> 00:33:34.573
And the alternative
to being an expert

00:33:34.573 --> 00:33:36.470
is, let the machine
worry about that.

00:33:36.470 --> 00:33:38.700
Because then you can
go on to doing things

00:33:38.700 --> 00:33:41.200
that I think are higher value,
which is what problems should

00:33:41.200 --> 00:33:44.400
we be solving, what work
should we prioritize,

00:33:44.400 --> 00:33:45.890
which new model should we build?

00:33:45.890 --> 00:33:49.104
And let the platform
do the fine-tuning.

00:33:49.104 --> 00:33:51.020
I think we've seen the
same thing, by the way,

00:33:51.020 --> 00:33:52.090
with feature engineering.

00:33:52.090 --> 00:33:54.830
And I've had discussions
with data scientists--

00:33:54.830 --> 00:33:56.990
even Googlers, data
scientists-- who

00:33:56.990 --> 00:34:00.080
told me that they think their
job is getting automated away.

00:34:00.080 --> 00:34:02.684
Because their job used to
be to look at some raw data,

00:34:02.684 --> 00:34:04.100
and then they could
figure out how

00:34:04.100 --> 00:34:06.420
to encode the raw
data in these features

00:34:06.420 --> 00:34:08.420
that would work well in
a machine-learned model.

00:34:08.420 --> 00:34:10.400
And now, with deep
neural networks,

00:34:10.400 --> 00:34:12.690
the network does
most of the encoding.

00:34:12.690 --> 00:34:15.080
And it does a better job
of encoding the raw input

00:34:15.080 --> 00:34:18.620
than most data scientists do,
so that the data scientist's

00:34:18.620 --> 00:34:20.429
job is no longer to
be a feature engineer.

00:34:20.429 --> 00:34:22.970
And I think the next phase is
the data scientist's job is not

00:34:22.970 --> 00:34:25.187
going to be to be
choosing hyperparameters,

00:34:25.187 --> 00:34:27.020
and then it's probably
going to be not to be

00:34:27.020 --> 00:34:28.977
choosing model architectures.

00:34:28.977 --> 00:34:30.560
MELANIE WARRICK:
Well, and that starts

00:34:30.560 --> 00:34:33.320
to touch into Auto ML too,
which-- have you explored

00:34:33.320 --> 00:34:33.852
that yet?

00:34:33.852 --> 00:34:35.060
SIVAN ALDOR-NOIMAN: A little.

00:34:35.060 --> 00:34:35.851
ERIK ANDREJKO: Yes.

00:34:35.851 --> 00:34:37.837
That's on the list of
things we have used.

00:34:37.837 --> 00:34:38.591
Yeah.

00:34:38.591 --> 00:34:40.549
SIVAN ALDOR-NOIMAN:
[LAUGHS] We've done-- like,

00:34:40.549 --> 00:34:41.630
we tried once or twice.

00:34:41.630 --> 00:34:43.520
There might be good
use cases for it,

00:34:43.520 --> 00:34:46.070
and I agree that some
automation is good.

00:34:46.070 --> 00:34:48.199
But maybe my own
internal thing is,

00:34:48.199 --> 00:34:49.670
like, I want to see the model.

00:34:49.670 --> 00:34:54.770
I need to see the actual
architecture and what it does.

00:34:54.770 --> 00:34:57.740
And I believe in the
technology of deep learning.

00:34:57.740 --> 00:34:59.540
I understand it mathematically.

00:34:59.540 --> 00:35:01.050
I understand where it's going.

00:35:01.050 --> 00:35:04.430
But I also believe that the
value of a data scientist

00:35:04.430 --> 00:35:07.410
is in interpreting some
of these things sometimes,

00:35:07.410 --> 00:35:10.910
and the more deep learning
is applied the less

00:35:10.910 --> 00:35:12.512
people have an understanding--

00:35:12.512 --> 00:35:14.720
even the people who actually
developed these models--

00:35:14.720 --> 00:35:17.280
have a less understanding of
what's going on internally,

00:35:17.280 --> 00:35:19.680
what are the actual
features it's creating,

00:35:19.680 --> 00:35:22.500
how to explain it to anyone.

00:35:22.500 --> 00:35:24.960
The causal things
that people like

00:35:24.960 --> 00:35:28.240
to interpret with causality
becomes really hard.

00:35:28.240 --> 00:35:32.820
So I think with Auto ML it's
even more obscure than normal.

00:35:32.820 --> 00:35:35.851
I see a lot of benefit
for certain places,

00:35:35.851 --> 00:35:37.350
especially if you
want to accelerate

00:35:37.350 --> 00:35:39.780
certain things that are
very common tasks in machine

00:35:39.780 --> 00:35:40.440
learning.

00:35:40.440 --> 00:35:42.900
Then you should use it, because
there's-- you don't need

00:35:42.900 --> 00:35:45.690
to hire today, like, a whole
suite of data scientists just

00:35:45.690 --> 00:35:47.250
to build a model.

00:35:47.250 --> 00:35:49.680
That's already kind of
implemented for you.

00:35:49.680 --> 00:35:52.230
And it takes away that
magic of feature selection

00:35:52.230 --> 00:35:53.240
and all of these things.

00:35:53.240 --> 00:35:55.573
So I think it's very useful
for those kind of use cases.

00:35:55.573 --> 00:35:57.420
If you have a data
scientist and they

00:35:57.420 --> 00:35:59.760
know what they're doing,
letting them play around

00:35:59.760 --> 00:36:02.190
with TensorFlow and building
models that you can better

00:36:02.190 --> 00:36:04.690
grasp when they fail
and when they're good,

00:36:04.690 --> 00:36:06.070
it's good for the organization.

00:36:06.070 --> 00:36:10.276
So this is why, I guess, Erik
and I are a good pairing.

00:36:10.276 --> 00:36:12.900
He pushes in that direction, and
I push in the other direction.

00:36:12.900 --> 00:36:14.252
So on average it's fine.

00:36:14.252 --> 00:36:15.210
MARK MANDEL: Excellent.

00:36:15.210 --> 00:36:17.710
MELANIE WARRICK: Well, and one
other thing on the TPU side--

00:36:17.710 --> 00:36:21.660
do you combine those with GPUs
and CPUs in the calculations,

00:36:21.660 --> 00:36:23.710
in the computations
that you're doing?

00:36:23.710 --> 00:36:23.970
ERIK ANDREJKO: Yeah.

00:36:23.970 --> 00:36:25.410
That's one thing
that I would say

00:36:25.410 --> 00:36:27.570
is still a challenge
for us, which

00:36:27.570 --> 00:36:29.850
is when you have a
particular model training

00:36:29.850 --> 00:36:33.190
job, having to decide the
compute infrastructure.

00:36:33.190 --> 00:36:35.460
Because sometimes
it's actually--

00:36:35.460 --> 00:36:39.150
especially in dollar
terms, that requires a lot

00:36:39.150 --> 00:36:41.400
of trial and error on our part.

00:36:41.400 --> 00:36:43.530
I'd love to be able
to just set a budget.

00:36:43.530 --> 00:36:45.930
I would like to train this
model for this many dollars

00:36:45.930 --> 00:36:49.050
and be able to trade off time
to conversions and dollars.

00:36:49.050 --> 00:36:50.460
MELANIE WARRICK: Oh, that sounds
like a good product to build.

00:36:50.460 --> 00:36:51.147
ERIK ANDREJKO: Yeah.

00:36:51.147 --> 00:36:52.430
SIVAN ALDOR-NOIMAN:
A forecaster.

00:36:52.430 --> 00:36:53.263
ERIK ANDREJKO: Yeah.

00:36:53.263 --> 00:36:55.270
Because what we do now
is we'll run a job,

00:36:55.270 --> 00:36:57.660
and we'll use just
distributed CPU training,

00:36:57.660 --> 00:37:00.060
and then we'll compare
that with a single GPU,

00:37:00.060 --> 00:37:02.880
and then you can choose
your flavors of GPUs or TPU.

00:37:02.880 --> 00:37:04.980
And then we have to
decide, for that class

00:37:04.980 --> 00:37:07.200
of models, which one
